{
  "sections": [
    {
      "role": "QUAL",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The primary experimental results are reported using 'Loss' values rather than standard classification metrics like Accuracy or F1-score, making it impossible to directly compare performance with the cited baselines or assess the practical utility of the model.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Report Accuracy and F1-scores for all datasets and baselines."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper reports single-point estimates for all results without any indication of variance, standard deviation, or confidence intervals across multiple runs.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Conduct experiments with multiple random seeds (e.g., 5-10) and report the mean and standard deviation of the performance metrics to demonstrate statistical significance and robustness."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The comparison is limited to the 2015 Zhang & LeCun baseline and traditional non-deep learning methods, lacking comparison against more recent or state-of-the-art character-level CNN variants or other efficient encoding methods that have emerged since 2015.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Include comparisons with more recent character-level CNN architectures or other efficient embedding techniques to better contextualize the proposed method's performance."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper does not mention code availability, and while hyperparameters are listed, details on data preprocessing (beyond lowercasing) and specific dataset splits are sparse, hindering full reproducibility.",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-006",
            "SRC-007"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Provide a link to the code repository and a detailed appendix specifying exact dataset splits, preprocessing steps, and any other implementation details necessary for reproduction."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The claim of 'competitive accuracies' is not supported by the provided loss values, which are often higher (worse) than the Zhang & LeCun baselines in several datasets (e.g., Yelp Polarity: 7.96 vs 5.89; Yahoo Answers: 31.90 vs 29.55).",
          "source_roles": [
            "QUAL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Clarify the relationship between the reported loss and actual classification accuracy, and revise the claims to reflect the trade-off between efficiency and performance if accuracy is not competitive."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "CLAR",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The writing quality is poor, with frequent grammatical errors, awkward phrasing, and inconsistent terminology that hinder readability and professional presentation.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-001",
            "SRC-003",
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Perform a thorough line-by-line proofreading to correct grammatical errors such as 'reduces drastically' and 'In all of ours experiments'."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The algorithm presentation lacks formal pseudocode, relying instead on prose and examples, which makes the encoding procedure less precise and harder to reproduce without ambiguity.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-003",
            "SRC-004"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Provide a clear, step-by-step pseudocode block for the encoding algorithm, explicitly defining inputs, outputs, and edge cases such as the handling of characters not in the rank list."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "Mathematical notation is inconsistent and poorly defined, as the paper uses 'rank' to denote frequency position but does not formally define the mapping function or the vector dimensions in a consistent mathematical framework.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-004",
            "SRC-006"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Define the encoding function formally and specify the relationship between the character rank and the code length, clarifying whether the rank is 0-indexed or 1-indexed."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "Tables are poorly formatted and contain ambiguous abbreviations, such as cryptic column headers in Table 6 (e.g., 'YLP P', 'AMZ F') that are not fully defined, and Table 4 uses 'merge_1' without specifying the type of merge operation.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Expand the abbreviations in Table 6 or provide a clear legend, and specify the type of merge operation (e.g., concatenation, addition) used in the network architecture."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper lacks a clear definition of the 'compressing inspired techniques' and does not explicitly state the name of the algorithm (Tagged Huffman Code) until the Discussion section, which delays the reader's understanding of the core contribution.",
          "source_roles": [
            "CLAR"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-001",
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Introduce the specific compression technique (Tagged Huffman Code) in the Introduction or early in Section 2 and explain the connection between the proposed encoding and Huffman coding more clearly."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "SIGN",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The work addresses a broadly important problem in NLP: the computational inefficiency and high resource requirements of character-level deep learning models, which limits their accessibility to researchers and practitioners without high-end hardware.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-001",
            "SRC-010"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The method is validated on a diverse set of standard, large-scale text classification datasets (Ag News, Sogou News, DBpedia, Yelp, Yahoo Answers, Amazon), demonstrating generalizability across different domains and languages (including Chinese via Pinyin).",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-007",
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "While the paper claims 'competitive accuracies,' the results in Table 6 show that the proposed method often underperforms the state-of-the-art baseline (Zhang et al. 2015) in terms of loss, indicating that the improvement is primarily in efficiency (time/parameters) rather than accuracy, with accuracy degraded in several cases.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Explicitly discuss the trade-off between efficiency and accuracy, and provide a more nuanced analysis of why accuracy drops on certain datasets like Yelp Polarity and Yahoo Answers."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper highlights significant practical and societal benefits by enabling text classification on simpler hardware (e.g., NVIDIA 930M), which increases accessibility for students, startups, and resource-constrained environments, and mentions potential benefits for handling unseen words and typos.",
          "source_roles": [
            "SIGN"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-010",
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "ORIG",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The paper presents a creative combination of a traditional data compression technique (Tagged Huffman Code) with deep learning input representation, offering a distinct alternative to standard one-hot or embedding-based character encodings.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-003",
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        },
        {
          "statement": "The proposed encoding method is a straightforward adaptation of an existing compression algorithm (Silva de Moura et al., 2000) with minor modifications (using one '0' instead of two), lacking significant algorithmic novelty in the context of neural network input design.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-011"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "More rigorously justify why this specific compression scheme is superior to other dimensionality reduction techniques (e.g., hashing, PCA) for CNN inputs, beyond just parameter count."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The analysis of why the compact encoding works (or fails) in the context of convolutional feature extraction is superficial, relying on empirical results without providing theoretical or mechanistic insights into how the binary structure interacts with convolutional filters.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-009",
            "SRC-010"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Include an analysis of the learned convolutional filters to understand how they process the binary encoded sequences, or provide a theoretical argument for why this encoding preserves relevant linguistic features."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The experimental setup relies entirely on standard datasets from Zhang & LeCun (2015) and does not introduce new benchmarks or evaluation protocols specifically designed to test the limits or advantages of compact representations.",
          "source_roles": [
            "ORIG"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-007",
            "SRC-008"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Consider introducing a new benchmark or evaluation metric that specifically measures the efficiency-accuracy trade-off of compact encodings, or test on datasets with high out-of-vocabulary rates to better demonstrate the claimed robustness."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "role": "POL",
      "opinions": [
        {
          "statement": "The paper violates double-blind review policies by including specific acknowledgments that reveal the authors' institutional affiliations and funding sources.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-012"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Remove the 'ACKNOWLEDGMENTS' section or redact specific funding bodies (e.g., CAPES) and corporate donors (e.g., NVIDIA) that can be used to identify the authors or their institutions."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper lacks a dedicated 'Broader Impact' or 'Ethical Considerations' section, which is a standard requirement for ICLR submissions.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-012"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Add a section discussing the societal implications of the work, including potential benefits (e.g., accessibility for low-resource hardware) and risks (e.g., misuse in spam filtering or content moderation)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper does not discuss potential ethical risks or biases associated with the text classification task, such as the reinforcement of stereotypes or the impact on marginalized groups.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-009"
          ],
          "suggestions": [
            "Include a discussion on the ethical considerations of text classification, particularly regarding fairness and bias in the datasets and models."
          ]
        },
        {
          "statement": "The paper properly cites prior work, including the foundational work of Zhang & LeCun (2015) and the compression technique of Silva de Moura et al. (2000), indicating no evidence of plagiarism or dual submission.",
          "source_roles": [
            "POL"
          ],
          "evidence_anchors": [
            "SRC-013"
          ],
          "suggestions": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "recommendation": "Weak Reject"
}

